arted collecting those news  personal experiences were not being  featured in bangladesh strictly  regulated media  as part of a scholarship pretty began to  focus on them and to show people's  stories in her pop-up photo studio in  dhaka  i wanted to make  something  maybe  more focused on  trauma or more focus on  pain  individual individually you know in  newspaper and document daily maybe so  many cases is coming one after another  but i wanted to make it focus on  individual incident  the plight of working-class people is at  the center of british torn series  the lockdown cast them into an  existential crisis from one day to the  next  [Music]  when the  lockdown started  then  we found that  this situation very much extreme for the  working class people we knew that that  is we knew that  these things exist  visible but we are very ignorant  so  that time i first experienced that  someone is  coming in front my road and asking for  rice  and working class people they don't know  like they work on daily basis and they  get payment and they live their life  like this they don't have any savings  one of mother  she just  like sold her hair  to get some rice  they  like this kind of people they suffered a  lot  and  they are starting telling that it's  better to die with corona rather than  hunger  when bangladesh's garment factories  reopened with a coronavirus still  prevalent workers were suddenly faced  with a new kind of problem  when they  stay in the city they have to rent a  place you know and  small place  lots of people staying together because  they cannot afford to be alone in the  city and afford one room so in our one  room during the kobe time also when  everybody is talking about isolation if  you go to the slum how could we manage  the isolation  pretty blames bangladesh's society as  well as the government's poor covert  policies for people's needless suffering  during the pandemic i  really need one of the policemen he  did suicide  when he got